This is the classic exercism forth exercise. Let's implement it. Key points:

- Numbers: a sequence of one or more digits (possibly with a leading `-`). A word is a sequence of one or more characters that is not a number.
- Words are case-insensitive.
- Built-in words: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `dup`, `drop`, `swap`, `over`.
- Definition: `: word-name definition ;` — when executing `:`, the next token is the name, then everything up to `;` is the definition (tokens).

Tricky test cases:
- "can use different words with the same name": `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;`, `: foo 6 ;`, `bar foo` → `[5, 6]`. This means that when defining a word, the tokens of its definition must be resolved at definition time (immediately) — that is, `bar` captures the current meaning of `foo` (which pushes 5) at definition time. This is the "local scope" test. So we must resolve tokens to their current meaning when defining.

- "can define word that uses word with the same name": `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;`, `foo` → `[11]`. When defining the new `foo`, the token `foo` inside the definition refers to the old `foo` (which pushes 10). So resolve at definition time.

- "cannot redefine non-negative numbers": `: 1 2 ;` → error. So if the word name after `:` is a number, error.

- "errors if executing a non-existent word": `foo` → error "undefined operation".

So the design: a dictionary mapping from word name (lowercased string) to a compiled token list (resolved at definition time). Each token is either a number (push) or a reference to a dictionary entry (a list of resolved tokens).

